<exercisegroup>
<title>Isolating via matrix algebra</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case,
	use matrix algebra to isolate and determine the unknown variable matrix.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
			4 \amp  1 \\
			7 \amp -8
		\end{abmatrix}
		-
		\begin{bmatrix}
			1 \amp 3 \\
			1 \amp 2
		\end{bmatrix}
		X
		=
		\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
			 0 \amp 6 \\
			-6 \amp 1
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		It's less messy to perform algebraic manipulations with letters,
		so assign
		<md><mrow>
			A \amp =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				4 \amp  1 \\
				7 \amp -8
			\end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\amp
			B \amp =
			\begin{bmatrix}
				1 \amp 3 \\
				1 \amp 2
			\end{bmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\amp
			C \amp =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				 0 \amp 6 \\
				-6 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</mrow></md>
		Then we have
		<md alignment="gather">
			<mrow> A - B X = C </mrow>
			<mrow> A - C = B X </mrow>
			<mrow> \inv{B} (A - C) = \inv{B} B X </mrow>
			<mrow> \inv{B} (A - C) = I X </mrow>
			<mrow> X = \inv{B} (A - C) </mrow>
		</md>,
		assuming that <m>B</m> is invertible.
		Applying <xref ref="proposition-inverses-2x2-inverse" />,
		for <m>B</m> we have
		<md> a d - b c = 1 \cdot 2 - 3 \cdot 1 = -1 </md>,
		and so <m>B</m> is indeed invertible,
		with inverse
		<md>
			\inv{B} =
			\frac{1}{-1}
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				 2 \amp -3 \\
				-1 \amp  1
			\end{abmatrix}
			=
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				-2 \amp  3 \\
				 1 \amp -1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
		With this inverse in hand,
		we may calculate
		<md>
			<mrow>
				A - C \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					4 \amp  1 \\
					7 \amp -8
				\end{abmatrix}
				-
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					 0 \amp 6 \\
					-6 \amp 1
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					 4 \amp -5 \\
					13 \amp -9
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

		</md>,
		<md>

			<mrow> X \amp = \inv{B} (A - C) </mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					-2 \amp  3 \\
					 1 \amp -1
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					 4 \amp -5 \\
					13 \amp -9
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					31 \amp -17 \\
					-9 \amp   4
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\utrans{X}
		\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
			 1 \amp -3 \\
			-3 \amp 10
		\end{abmatrix}
		+
		\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
			3 \amp 1 \\
			3 \amp -3
		\end{abmatrix}
		=
		I
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution>

		<p>
		It's less messy to perform algebraic manipulations with letters,
		so assign
		<md><mrow>
			A \amp =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				 1 \amp -3 \\
				-3 \amp 10
			\end{abmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\amp
			B \amp =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				3 \amp 1 \\
				3 \amp -3
			\end{abmatrix}
			\text{.}
		</mrow></md>
		Then we have
		<md alignment="gather">
			<mrow> \utrans{X} A + B = I </mrow>
			<mrow> \utrans{X} A = I - B </mrow>
			<mrow> \utrans{X} A \inv{A} = (I - B) \inv{A} </mrow>
			<mrow> \utrans{X} I = (I - B) \inv{A} </mrow>
			<mrow> \utrans{X} = (I - B) \inv{A} </mrow>
		</md>,
		assuming that <m>A</m> is invertible.
		</p>

		<p>
		<em>Note how we have multiplied by <m>\inv{A}</m> <alert>on the right</alert> on both sides of the second equality above to obtain the third.</em>
		Since <alert>order of matrix multiplication matters</alert>,
		multiplying by <m>\inv{A}</m> on the left would <em>not</em> cancel the <m>A</m> on the left-hand side of the equality.
		</p>

		<p>
		The final algebraic step is to <q>undo</q> the transpose that has been applied to <m>X</m>,
		but by <xref ref="proposition-matrix-ops-algebra-rules-transpose-transpose">Rule</xref>
		of <xref ref="proposition-matrix-ops-algebra-rules" />,
		transpose reverses itself:
		<md alignment="gather">
			<mrow> \utrans{X} = (I - B) \inv{A} </mrow>
			<mrow> \utrans{(\utrans{X})} = \utrans{\bbrac{(I - B) \inv{A}}} </mrow>
			<mrow> X = \utrans{\bbrac{(I - B) \inv{A}}} </mrow>
		</md>.
		(We could apply more <xref ref="proposition-matrix-ops-algebra-rules-transpose" text="title" />
		from <xref ref="proposition-matrix-ops-algebra-rules" />
		to further <q>simplify</q> the right-hand side of the last line above,
		but it's probably easier to just calculate one final transpose at the end instead of several intermediate transposes along the way.)
		</p>

		<p>
		Our first calculation should be to confirm that <m>A</m> is invertible.
		Applying <xref ref="proposition-inverses-2x2-inverse" />,
		for <m>A</m> we have
		<md> a d - b c = 1 \cdot 10 - (-3) \cdot (-3) = 1 </md>,
		and so <m>A</m> is indeed invertible,
		with inverse
		<md>
			\inv{A} =
			\begin{bmatrix}
				10 \amp 3 \\
				 3 \amp 1
			\end{bmatrix}
		</md>.
		With this inverse in hand,
		and noting that the <m>I</m> in our equality should be the <m>2 \times 2</m> <xref ref="definition-inverses-identity-matrix" text="title" /> to match the dimensions of the other matrices involved,
		we may calculate
		<md>

			<mrow>
				I - B \amp =
				\bidentmattwo
				-
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					3 \amp 1 \\
					3 \amp -3
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					-2 \amp -1 \\
					-3 \amp  4
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

		</md>,
		<md>

			<mrow>
				(I - B) \inv{A} \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					-2 \amp -1 \\
					-3 \amp  4
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{bmatrix}
					10 \amp 3 \\
					 3 \amp 1
				\end{bmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					-23 \amp -7 \\
					-18 \amp -5
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

		</md>,
		<md>

			<mrow> X \amp = \utrans{\bbrac{(I - B) \inv{A}}} </mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\utrans{
					\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
						-23 \amp -7 \\
						-18 \amp -5
					\end{abmatrix}
				}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					-23 \amp -18 \\
					- 7 \amp - 5
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

		</md>.
		</p>

	</solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement component="html"><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
			1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp  2 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp  1 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
			1 \amp 4 \amp  2 \amp -1
		\end{abmatrix}
		X
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
			 4 \amp -11 \amp 14 \amp -3 \\
			-1 \amp   3 \amp -4 \amp  1 \\
			 0 \amp   0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
			 0 \amp   1 \amp  0 \amp  0
		\end{abmatrix}
		- 5 I
		=
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
			-8 \amp 2 \amp -9 \amp  7 \\
			 7 \amp 1 \amp  3 \amp -5 \\
			 2 \amp 6 \amp  6 \amp -4 \\
			-8 \amp 1 \amp  9 \amp  3
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<statement component="latex"><p><m>
		\left[\begin{smallmatrix}
			1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp  2 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp  1 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
			1 \amp 4 \amp  2 \amp -1
		\end{smallmatrix}\right]
		X
		\left[\begin{smallmatrix}
			 4 \amp -11 \amp 14 \amp -3 \\
			-1 \amp   3 \amp -4 \amp  1 \\
			 0 \amp   0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
			 0 \amp   1 \amp  0 \amp  0
		\end{smallmatrix}\right]
		- 5 I
		=
		\left[\begin{smallmatrix}
			-8 \amp 2 \amp -9 \amp  7 \\
			 7 \amp 1 \amp  3 \amp -5 \\
			 2 \amp 6 \amp  6 \amp -4 \\
			-8 \amp 1 \amp  9 \amp  3
		\end{smallmatrix}\right]
	</m></p></statement>

	<hint><p> First see <xref ref="exercise-inverses-verify-4x4" text="type-local" />. </p></hint>

	<solution>

		<p>
		It's less messy to perform algebraic manipulations with letters,
		so assign
		<md>
			<mrow>
				A \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp  2 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp  1 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
					1 \amp 4 \amp  2 \amp -1
				\end{abmatrix}
				\text{,}
			</mrow>
			<mrow>
				B \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					 4 \amp -11 \amp 14 \amp -3 \\
					-1 \amp   3 \amp -4 \amp  1 \\
					 0 \amp   0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
					 0 \amp   1 \amp  0 \amp  0
				\end{abmatrix}
				\text{,}
			</mrow>
			<mrow>
				C \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					-8 \amp 2 \amp -9 \amp  7 \\
					 7 \amp 1 \amp  3 \amp -5 \\
					 2 \amp 6 \amp  6 \amp -4 \\
					-8 \amp 1 \amp  9 \amp  3
				\end{abmatrix}
				\text{.}
			</mrow>
		</md>
		Then we have
		<md alignment="gather">
			<mrow> A X B - 5 I = C </mrow>
			<mrow> A X B = C + 5 I </mrow>
			<mrow> \inv{A} (A X B) \inv{B} = \inv{A} (C + 5 I) \inv{B} </mrow>
			<mrow> (\inv{A} A) X (B \inv{B}) = \inv{A} (C + 5 I) \inv{B} </mrow>
			<mrow> I X I = \inv{A} (C + 5 I) \inv{B} </mrow>
			<mrow> X = \inv{A} (C + 5 I) \inv{B} </mrow>
		</md>,
		assuming that both <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are invertible.
		</p>

		<p>
		Remember that <alert>order of matrix multiplication matters</alert>.
		In particular,
		we <em>cannot</em> rearrange <m>A X B</m> to either <m>A B X</m> or <m>X A B</m>,
		and we have been careful to cancel <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> by multiplying by <m>\inv{A}</m> <em>on the left</em> and by <m>\inv{B}</m> <em>on the right</em> on both sides of the equality.
		</p>

		<p>
		Now back to the question of the invertibility of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
		Per the hint,
		matrices <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> in this exercise match the matrices in <xref ref="exercise-inverses-verify-4x4" />,
		where we verified that they are inverses of each other;
		that is,
		<md><mrow> \inv{A} \amp = B \text{,} \amp \inv{B} \amp = A \text{.} </mrow></md>
		While normally there is no calculation advantage to expanding the brackets in our last algebraic expression above,
		in this case we might be able to further simplify our right-hand side a bit more:
		<md alignment="gather">
			<mrow> X = \inv{A} (C + 5 I) \inv{B} </mrow>
			<mrow> X = \inv{A} (C + 5 I) A </mrow>
			<mrow> X = \inv{A} C A + 5 \inv{A} I A </mrow>
			<mrow> X = \inv{A} C A + 5 \inv{A} A </mrow>
			<mrow> X = \inv{A} C A + 5 I </mrow>
			<mrow> X = B C A + 5 I </mrow>
		</md>.
		Once again, remember that <alert>order of matrix multiplication matters</alert>,
		and so while <m>\inv{A} I A = I</m> is true because first we may simplify <m>I A = A</m> before <q>cancelling</q> <m>A</m> with its inverse,
		we <em>cannot</em> similarly simplify <m>\inv{A} C A</m> because the <m>A</m> is not right next to its inverse to <q>cancel.</q>
		</p>

		<p>
		Our final algebraic expression above now tells us what to calculate:
		<md>

			<mrow>
				B C \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					 4 \amp -11 \amp 14 \amp -3 \\
					-1 \amp   3 \amp -4 \amp  1 \\
					 0 \amp   0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
					 0 \amp   1 \amp  0 \amp  0
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					-8 \amp 2 \amp -9 \amp  7 \\
					 7 \amp 1 \amp  3 \amp -5 \\
					 2 \amp 6 \amp  6 \amp -4 \\
					-8 \amp 1 \amp  9 \amp  3
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					-57 \amp  78 \amp -12 \amp 18 \\
					 13 \amp -22 \amp   3 \amp -3 \\
					  2 \amp   6 \amp   6 \amp -4 \\
					  7 \amp   1 \amp   3 \amp -5
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

		</md>,
		<md>

			<mrow> B C A \amp = (B C) A </mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					-57 \amp  78 \amp -12 \amp 18 \\
					 13 \amp -22 \amp   3 \amp -3 \\
					  2 \amp   6 \amp   6 \amp -4 \\
					  7 \amp   1 \amp   3 \amp -5
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp  2 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp  1 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
					1 \amp 4 \amp  2 \amp -1
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					-39 \amp -99 \amp 138 \amp -54 \\
					 10 \amp  27 \amp -29 \amp   7 \\
					- 2 \amp -10 \amp - 6 \amp  14 \\
					  2 \amp   1 \amp -21 \amp  20
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

		</md>.
		Taking the <m>I</m> in the equality to represent the <m>4 \times 4</m> <xref ref="definition-inverses-identity-matrix" text="title" /> to match the dimensions of the other matrices,
		we also have
		<md>
			5 I =
			5 \bidentmatfour =
			\begin{bmatrix}
				5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \\
			\end{bmatrix}
		</md>.
		Finally,
		<md>

			<mrow> X \amp = B C A + 5 I </mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					-39 \amp -99 \amp 138 \amp -54 \\
					 10 \amp  27 \amp -29 \amp   7 \\
					- 2 \amp -10 \amp - 6 \amp  14 \\
					  2 \amp   1 \amp -21 \amp  20
				\end{abmatrix}
				+
				\begin{bmatrix}
					5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
					0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \\
				\end{bmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					-34 \amp -99 \amp 138 \amp -54 \\
					 10 \amp  32 \amp -29 \amp   7 \\
					- 2 \amp -10 \amp - 1 \amp  14 \\
					  2 \amp   1 \amp -21 \amp  25
				\end{abmatrix}
			</mrow>

		</md>.
		</p>

	</solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
